docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/codex-audit-placement-v2-r1.log:32:File each statement under the narrowest question that names its matter, and under that one only; never add a broader question beside it. A company-wide figure question (total revenue, segment revenue, gross margin, operating margin, profit, capex) takes only the company's own reported figures, guidance, estimates of that figure and management's statements about it. News about a customer, supplier, competitor, product, policy or deal is filed under the question that names that matter (an event, a policy process, a theme), or under none when no question names it. A rumour is filed exactly as a confirmed statement is.
docs/research/2026-10-31-reference-context/codex-audit-placement-v2-r1.log:34:File each statement under the question whose answer it makes more or less likely, the narrowest such question, and under that one only; never add a broader question beside it. A question about a product generation takes the steps, dates, delays, cancellations and configuration changes of that generation and of its components (a chip, rack, board or interconnect built for it), and named customers' statements about receiving it. A company-wide figure question (total revenue, segment revenue, gross margin, operating margin, profit, capex) takes the company's own reported figures, guidance and estimates of that figure, management's statements about it, and a named counterparty's order, plan, cut or price with a size that bears on it directly; a general statement that demand is strong or supply is tight is filed under the question that names that driver (demand, supply, share, pricing) when one is open, else under none. A question about a share, a price or an input takes figures, estimates and named parties' actions about that share, price or input, in any market the question covers. News about a customer, supplier, competitor, product, policy or deal with none of these homes is filed under the question that names that matter (an event, a policy process, a theme), or under none. A rumour is filed exactly as a confirmed statement is.
